    {
      "id": "P39",
      "verified": true,
      "selfContained": true,
      "sourceSupported": true,
      "hardReasoning": true,
      "question": "Select all statements that are correct about the pressure in the hydraulic cylinder and the work done by the gas when a 24 kg student sits on the chair (total mass 30 kg) causing a 12 cm displacement, using the given piston area and atmospheric pressure.",
      "solution": "Compute the total downward force: $F = (m_{\\text{student}} + m_{\\text{seat}})\\,g = (24\\,\\text{kg}+6\\,\\text{kg})\\times10\\,\\text{m/s}^2 = 300\\,\\text{N}$.\\nThe piston cross‑sectional area is $S = 30\\,\\text{cm}^2 = 30\\times10^{-4}\\,\\text{m}^2 = 3\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^2$.\\nPressure increase due to the load: $\\Delta p = \\frac{F}{S}=\\frac{300\\,\\text{N}}{3\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^2}=1\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$.\\nAbsolute pressure after loading: $p_{\\text{final}} = p_0 + \\Delta p = 1\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}+1\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=2\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$.\\nVolume change of the gas: $\\Delta V = S\\,\\Delta h = 3\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^2 \\times 0.12\\,\\text{m}=3.6\\times10^{-4}\\,\\text{m}^3$.\\nAssuming the pressure varies linearly from $p_0$ to $p_{\\text{final}}$, the average pressure during compression is $p_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{p_0 + p_{\\text{final}}}{2}=1.5\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$.\\nWork done by the gas (negative because the gas is compressed): $W = -p_{\\text{avg}}\\,\\Delta V = -1.5\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\times3.6\\times10^{-4}\\,\\text{m}^3 \\approx -54\\,\\text{J}$.\\nCross‑check: using $W = -\\int p\\,dV$ for an isothermal process gives the same order of magnitude.",
      "answer": "$p_{\\text{final}} = 2\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$; $W \\approx -54\\,\\text{J}$",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The absolute pressure in the cylinder after loading is $2\\times10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$.",
          "correct": true,
          "misconception": null
        },
        {
          "text": "The pressure increase in the cylinder is $5\\times10^{4}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$.",
          "correct": false,
          "misconception": "miscalculated pressure"
        },
        {
          "text": "The work done by the gas during compression is $-54\\,\\text{J}$.",
          "correct": true,
          "misconception": null
        },
        {
          "text": "The work done by the gas during compression is $+54\\,\\text{J}$.",
          "correct": false,
          "misconception": "sign error"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P40",
      "verified": true,
      "selfContained": true,
      "sourceSupported": true,
      "hardReasoning": false,
      "question": "When the temperature of the gas in the tire rises from 27 °C to 54 °C while the volume stays constant, the pressure becomes 5.45 atm.",
      "solution": "Convert temperatures to kelvin: $T_1 = 27\\,^{\\circ}\\text{C}+273 = 300\\,\\text{K}$, $T_2 = 54\\,^{\\circ}\\text{C}+273 = 327\\,\\text{K}$.\\nFor a constant volume, $\\frac{P_1}{T_1}=\\frac{P_2}{T_2}$, so $P_2 = P_1\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=5\\,\\text{atm}\\times\\frac{327}{300}\\approx5.45\\,\\text{atm}$.",
      "answer": "True",
      "claims": []
    },
    {
      "id": "P41",
      "verified": true,
      "selfContained": true,
      "sourceSupported": true,
      "hardReasoning": true,
      "question": "Select all statements that correctly describe the magnitude and sign of the work done by the gas when the piston moves from 30 cm to 35 cm in a cylinder of diameter 20 cm at constant pressure 102 kPa.",
      "solution": "Cylinder inner diameter $d = 20\\,\\text{cm}=0.20\\,\\text{m}$, so cross‑sectional area $A = \\pi\\left(\\frac{d}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi(0.10\\,\\text{m})^2 \\approx 3.14\\times10^{-2}\\,\\text{m}^2$.\\nDisplacement $\\Delta h = 35\\,\\text{cm}-30\\,\\text{cm}=5\\,\\text{cm}=0.05\\,\\text{m}$.\\nVolume change $\\Delta V = A\\,\\Delta h \\approx 3.14\\times10^{-2}\\,\\text{m}^2\\times0.05\\,\\text{m}=1.57\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^3$.\\nPressure $P = 102\\,\\text{kPa}=1.02\\times10^{5}\\,\\text{Pa}$.\\nWork done by the gas $W = P\\,\\Delta V \\approx 1.02\\times10^{5}\\,\\text{Pa}\\times1.57\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^3 \\approx 1.60\\times10^{2}\\,\\text{J}$. The work is positive because the gas expands and pushes the piston upward.",
      "answer": "$W \\approx +1.6\\times10^{2}\\,\\text{J}$",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The work done by the gas is $+1.6\\times10^{2}\\,\\text{J}$.",
          "correct": true,
          "misconception": null
        },
        {
          "text": "The magnitude of the work is $1.6\\times10^{2}\\,\\text{J}$.",
          "correct": true,
          "misconception": null
        },
        {
          "text": "The work done by the gas is $-0.16\\,\\text{J}$.",
          "correct": false,
          "misconception": "decimal misplacement"
        },
        {
          "text": "The work done by the gas is $-160\\,\\text{J}$.",
          "correct": false,
          "misconception": "sign error"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P42",
      "verified": true,
      "selfContained": true,
      "sourceSupported": true,
      "hardReasoning": false,
      "question": "Raising the pressure in a constant‑volume container from 4 atm to 4.5 atm increases the gas temperature from 70 °C to 113.6 °C.",
      "solution": "Initial temperature $T_1 = 70\\,^{\\circ}\\text{C}+273 = 343\\,\\text{K}$.\\nUsing $\\frac{P_1}{T_1}=\\frac{P_2}{T_2}$ with $P_1=4\\,\\text{atm}$ and $P_2=4.5\\,\\text{atm}$ gives $T_2 = T_1\\frac{P_2}{P_1}=343\\,\\text{K}\\times\\frac{4.5}{4}=386.6\\,\\text{K}$.\\nConvert back to Celsius: $T_2-273 = 386.6-273 \\approx 113.6\\,^{\\circ}\\text{C}$.",
      "answer": "True",
      "claims": []
    },
    {
      "id": "P43",
      "verified": true,
      "selfContained": true,
      "sourceSupported": true,
      "hardReasoning": false,
      "question": "Calculate the amount of heat rejected ($Q_2$) by the refrigerator when its coefficient of performance is 3 and the compressor does 180 kJ of work per cycle.",
      "solution": "COP is defined as $\\text{COP}=\\frac{Q_1}{A}$, so the heat absorbed from the cold space is $Q_1 = \\text{COP}\\times A = 3\\times180\\,\\text{kJ}=540\\,\\text{kJ}$.\\nEnergy balance for one cycle: $Q_2 = Q_1 + A = 540\\,\\text{kJ}+180\\,\\text{kJ}=720\\,\\text{kJ}$.",
      "answer": "$Q_2 = 720\\,\\text{kJ}$",
      "claims": []
    }
  ]
}
